# Changelog

All notable changes to this repository are documented in this file, with a
date on each entry.

The format follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.0.0/).

## [2.0] - 2026-08-31

- `python/verify_theorem_equiv_symbolic.py` - new: independent symbolic
  (SymPy) verification of the algebraic chain in the proof of Theorem
  5.3 (the v*e^v=e^(-1) substitution, the derivative at the fixed
  point, the Taylor expansion used in the transfer step). All four
  checks pass exactly.
- `pari/verify_critical_pari.gp` - new: independent recomputation of
  rho_m (Table 3) using native PARI/GP truncated power series
  arithmetic, block-indexed, agreeing with `critical_point.py` to
  within 3x10^-5.
- `README.md` - updated to list both additions.

### Changed - consolidation into the preprint
This release brings the repository into line with the paper as it now
stands: a single, self-contained preprint titled "Strict Monotonicity and
a Lambert-W Asymptotic for Growth Rates of Non-Plane Strict m-Gonal
Cacti" (DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.22206801). The paper now includes the full
proof of the transfer/asymptotic theorem (previously only cited from the
companion enumeration paper) and a new central result: a closed-form
large-m equivalent for 1-rho_m, with a Lambert-W constant, proved in full
(Theorem 5.3).

- `README.md` - rewritten to match the new title, scope, and file set;
  added the Citation section with the preprint's own DOI and this
  repository's DOI.
- `paper/` - added: LaTeX source and compiled PDF of the preprint.

### Added
- `python/verify_transfer_constants.py` - independent finite-difference
  recomputation of Phi_y and Phi_hh (Table 3), by a route genuinely
  different from the paper's closed-form derivation.
- `python/extended_range_m30.py` - extends the critical-data table and
  the check of the two-term asymptotic equivalent from m=12 to m=30,
  confirming the O((log m)/m^2) error term numerically over a much wider
  range than previously checked.

### Fixed
- `python/compute_rho.py` and `python/newton_rho.py` - both had a
  systematic bias (~10^-2) in their ratio-test estimate of rho_m, from
  omitting the n^(-3/2) polynomial correction factor present in the
  transfer theorem's asymptotic formula. Corrected; both now agree with
  `critical_point.py` to 10^-4-10^-5, a ~250x reduction in error.

### Notes
- Every script in `python/` and `coq/` now corresponds to a claim that
  appears, under the same theorem/table numbers, in the current preprint.
- Three components of the monotonicity argument remain machine-checked in
  Coq, unchanged from the previous release: this covers specific lemmas
  (Lemma 4.6, the operator-monotonicity step of Theorem 4.4, and an
  abstract involution-counting principle), not Theorem 4.7 in its
  entirety.

## [1.1] - 2026-08-07

### Changed
- `README.md` - added the Zenodo DOI of this repository (v1.0) to the
  Citation section, replacing the placeholder.
- `CHANGELOG.md` - this entry.

## [1.0] - 2026-08-07

### Added
- `coq/convexity_lemma.v` - Lemma 1 (§4), the analytic convexity lemma,
  proved via the mean value theorem.
- `coq/operator_monotonicity.v` - Lemma 2 (§4) in full, including the true
  infinite series (Coquelicot), not merely its elementary building blocks.
- `coq/involution_counting.v` - the abstract counting principle behind
  Theorem 2 (§5), generalized to any type with decidable equality and
  instantiated concretely for m = 5.
- `coq/coefficient_stabilization.v` - a finite-order computational
  confirmation, for m = 5 truncated at degree 13, that the Picard
  iteration defining the rooted series stabilizes.
- `python/` - the exact-rational series solver adapted from the companion
  enumeration paper (see README for the full reference), the symbolic
  (sympy) verification of the algebraic identities of §5-7, and the
  derivation of an explicit lower bound on rho_5.
- `README.md` - overview, rationale, scope, requirements, references,
  citation placeholder.

### Notes
- Preprint and Zenodo archive of the paper itself: not yet available (see
  README).
